1. what is Record classes?
   “A record class was introduced as a permanent feature in Java 16. It is used to represent data carriers, such as
   request and response DTOs. Records reduce boilerplate code by automatically generating a constructor, accessor
   methods, equals(), hashCode(), and toString(). Record components are final, so their references cannot be reassigned
   after construction.”

   Example:-
   public record EmployeeRequest(
       String name,
       int age
   ) {}
   Usage:
   EmployeeRequest request =
       new EmployeeRequest("Ravi", 30);

   System.out.println(request.name());
   System.out.println(request.age());
   Notice that we use request.name(), not request.getName().

   ✅Note:-The Java Compiler (javac) automatically generates the constructor, accessor methods, equals(), hashCode(), and
      toString() methods during compilation.
   ✅records are immutable in terms of their component references, but a mutable object inside a record can still be
   modified.
   Use a defensive copy with List.copyOf():
   record Employee(String name, List<String> skills) {
       public Employee {
           skills = List.copyOf(skills);
       }
   }

2. Can a Java record extend another class or have additional methods?
  “No, a record cannot extend another class because it implicitly extends java.lang.Record. However, a record can
  implement interfaces and contain additional methods, constructors, and static members.”

3. How to create immutable classes in Java
   Rule	Reason
   Declare class as final	                Prevents subclassing that could compromise immutability
   Make fields private final	            Prevents direct access and reassignment
   Initialize fields through constructor	Ensures fields are initialized once
   Don't provide setters	                Prevents changing field references through setters
   Use defensive copies	                    Prevents external code from modifying internal mutable objects

4. what are sealed classes?
   “Sealed classes were introduced as a permanent feature in Java 17 to control inheritance. We use the sealed keyword
   to restrict which classes can extend a class or which classes can implement an interface. The permits keyword
   specifies the permitted direct subclasses. Each permitted subclass must be declared as final, sealed, or non-sealed.”
   sealed class Payment permits CardPayment, UpiPayment {
   }

   final class CardPayment extends Payment {
   }

   non-sealed class UpiPayment extends Payment {
   }

   class GooglePay extends UpiPayment {
   }
   sealed — restricts direct subclasses.
   permits — lists the allowed direct subclasses.
   final — prevents further inheritance.
   non-sealed — allows unrestricted inheritance from that subclass.

5. Can an abstract class be a sealed class in Java? Why would we use both together?
   Yes, an abstract class can be a sealed class in Java
   Why do we use both together?
   - abstract prevents us from creating objects directly from the class.
   - sealed restricts which classes can extend it.
